{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-126325",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "First, let $f(y)=\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}$ and $g(y)=-\\frac{1}{2}y+1.$\n\nWe start by determining the points where the curves intersect by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =g(y) \\\\ \\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}} & =-\\frac{1}{2}y+1 \\\\ 4-{y}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{2}-y+1 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{4}{y}^{2}+y+3 & =0 \\\\ 5{y}^{2}-4y-12 & =0 \\\\ (5y+6)(y-2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution corresponding to our region is $y=2.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=2.$ \n\nNow, let's draw our usual horizontal arrow through the region to determine the left and right functions.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14224622/assets/122a21e72dc7473cc34ed3ece0325294eadcc86cbe8a5b91e4dfe4be77b369c0.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=g(y)=-\\frac{1}{2}y+1,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=f(y)=\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}.$\n\nTherefore, using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}y([\\text{right}]-[\\text{left}])\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y([\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}]-[-\\frac{1}{2}y+1])\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y(\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{2}y-1)\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
